WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.880 --> 00:00:01.670 align:start position:0%
 
hi  language learning is one of the most  enriching rewarding  satisfying activities we can engage in  if you're interested in this subject  please subscribe to my channel where i  talk about my experience in learning 15  languages  the most important task in language  learning in my opinion is the  acquisition of vocabulary words  if we have enough words we can make  sense of what we're reading or listening  to and we can somehow express ourselves  vocabulary is much more important than  grammar  the grammar you acquired gradually as  you become familiar with the language  with the words  but first of all you need the words so  how do we acquire vocabulary well i  think there's two sort of approaches  one is the deliberate study of  vocabulary by reading vocabulary lists  and trying to memorize them or doing  flash cards or these kinds of things  keeping handwritten lists  and the other is to learn through a lot  of exposure now  the strategy that you adopt will depend  on your personal preference and also on  in my view how much time you have if you  have a lot of time six seven hours a day  as i did when i was studying mandarin  chinese 45 years ago  then you can take an hour a day for the  deliberate study of vocabulary however  if you have one hour a day  and two thirds or three quarters of that  is time  which i call dead time in your car doing  tasks around the house walking the dog  then i suggest you don't try to  deliberately learn the vocabulary  and there's significant research which  shows that what they call block learning  or you take some material and try to  force yourself to learn it and review it  many many times and go through your for  example list of vocabulary or flash  cards over and over in the hope of  nailing that mastering it that is  relatively inefficient  and that interleaved learning  interleaving and i suggest you google  the term in other words where you come  across some information then you forget  it you go and look at some other  information you come back to that first  information so they're sort of  interleaving layers of different things  forgetting and relearning actually  enables you to learn things better  that  there's very quickly this the sort of uh  law of diminishing returns sets in when  we're trying deliberately to learn  something it's no longer fresh for our  brain and the brain basically pushes  back whereas if you forget and come back  to it you learn better so but if you  have six seven hours a day there's  nothing wrong with spending some time  reviewing flashcards but if i look at my  own pattern where i consume a lot  of of uh content through listening and  reading acquiring lots and lots of words  if i had to review them all in  flashcards or on lists i would spend my  whole you know i have to decide do i  want to spend my time going through uh  reviewing words say in flashcards or do  i want to spend that time listening and  reading to things of interest so i tend  to do the listening and reading i find  that i acquire words very quickly and i  have an enjoyable time doing it  of course speaking is also helpful  uh because  what you hear the native speakers say  while speaking  is what i call high resonance just as  interesting content is high resonance  and so you notice things  better  if you're engaged in a conversation and  you also notice when you weren't able to  find the words yourself and then you  hear someone else use them so that's  very very good however in my own case i  prefer to delay that  uh speaking situation unless there's a  need if i'm living in the country where  the language is spoken but otherwise i  prefer to delay it until i have  something meaningful to say and can  understand what the other person is  saying so that because otherwise we end  up with a very limited range of language  that we're exposed to like how are you  what's your name what's the weather like  and and so therefore again i prefer to  give myself that significant exposure  through listening and reading quite  confident that the high frequency words  will appear very often the medium  frequency words will appear less often  but i will eventually get them and the  very low frequency words  some mistake and some won't and if  they're that low frequency maybe i don't  really need them but ultimately the  choice is with the learner and my  preference is to study in an enjoyable  way uh if i were in a course somewhere  working five six hours a day having to  write an exam i might take a different  approach but there you have it that's my  take on  how to acquire vocabulary and i will  continue once a week with these  discussions on language learning and if  you're interested please subscribe to my  channel  bye for now

